    w1_story:
      "<b>What this is & how it works:</b> This is the classic 2D paper sheet: input <b>x</b> <i>('ex')</i> runs left-right, while output height <b>y = P(x)</b> <i>(spoken: 'why equals pee of ex')</i> runs up-down. Green dots where the curve pierces the horizontal X-axis (where height <b>y = 0</b>, <i>'why equals zero'</i>) are <b>real roots</b>. <b>Try it live:</b> grab the curve directly on the canvas or pull slider <b>a₀ (lift)</b> into positive values: when the curve lifts off the X-axis, the green dots vanish from the 1D rail — and immediately jump onto the vertical imaginary axis on the <b>Root Radar ℂ</b> and onto the glass floor in <b>3D Window #2</b>!",
